# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# scripts/scan-secrets.baseline — the known-and-accepted live identifiers
#
# Read by scripts/scan-secrets.sh.  One line per (path, value) pair the scan is
# allowed to see.  Anything the scan finds that is NOT listed here fails the
# gate.
#
# ── THIS FILE IS INTENTIONALLY EMPTY ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It holds zero entries, by design, and it should stay that way.  Nothing in
# this repository is exempt from the gate: not source, not templates, and not
# the run logs in docs/deployment.md.
#
# It used to hold seven entries under decision D3, which kept the original
# build's real account IDs, generated resource names, CloudFront domain and
# space/investigation ids in the run log on the grounds that they made the
# recorded evidence checkable.  **D3 is REVERSED on security-standards
# grounds.**  A live account identifier, a live generated resource name or a
# reachable endpoint does not belong in a tracked file, and "it is historical
# evidence" is not a standard anyone should be able to grant themselves an
# exception against.  Those values have been substituted, in place, with stable
# placeholders — the run logs are still readable as timelines and measurements,
# and every original value maps to one placeholder used consistently — so there
# is nothing left for this file to accept.
#
# Because it is empty, the gate is no longer load-bearing on it: deleting this
# file changes no scan outcome.  It stays because scripts/scan-secrets.sh reads
# it and the test suite covers its parsing, and because an empty file with this
# header is a stronger statement than an absent one.
#
# **Any new entry needs a justification nobody can wave through.**  An entry
# here switches the gate off for one exact value in one path, forever, and the
# reason it exists at all is that someone once found a plausible-sounding reason
# to commit live identifiers.  If you are reaching for this file, the answer is
# almost certainly to fix the source instead: write the run log with
# placeholders, or parameterise the code.  "The record needs the real value" is
# precisely the argument that was tried and rejected.
#
# Format, three `|`-separated fields:
#
#   <path glob> | <sha256 of the exact matched value> | <why it is accepted>
#
# Lines whose first non-blank character is `#` are comments.  Blank lines are
# ignored.  The path is matched with bash glob semantics against the
# repository-relative path, exactly like the scanner's exclusion patterns.
#
# Why a digest and not the value: a tracked file must not carry a fresh literal
# copy of a live identifier (that is the rule this gate exists to enforce), and
# a digest keeps the baseline diff-reviewable — a reviewer sees an entry appear,
# and the justification column has to earn it.  The digest is an integrity
# mechanism, not a secrecy one: an entry accepts one exact value, not a shape.
#
# ── Adding an entry (last resort) ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only for a value that genuinely cannot be placeheld, with a justification a
# reviewer would defend in a security review.  A live identifier in prose, in a
# run log, in code or in a template does not qualify — fix the source.  If you
# still need one:
#
#   1. Run the scan and copy the offending value from the `file:line:value`
#      finding:            scripts/scan-secrets.sh
#   2. Digest it exactly, with no trailing newline:
#                          printf '%s' '<value>' | shasum -a 256
#      (`sha256sum` or `openssl dgst -sha256` work too — the scanner accepts
#      whichever of the three is installed.)
#   3. Append one line here: the path the value may appear in, the digest, and
#      a one-line justification that names why the value cannot be a
#      placeholder.
#   4. Re-run `scripts/scan-secrets.sh` and confirm it exits 0.
#
# Removing an entry is always safe: the worst case is the gate failing until the
# value is scrubbed from the file, which is the outcome you want.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
